    "case_id": "601",
    "case_summary": "A 52-year-old woman presented with a mass on her left parotid gland causing otalgia. She did not report any constitutional symptoms initially. Eighteen months after initial presentation, the patient developed facial swelling, abdominal bloating, easy bruising, 30-lb weight gain. Past medical history was unremarkable.\n\nInitial examination: Blood pressure was 120/78 mm Hg, normal. Later examination (18 months): Blood pressure elevated to 193/113 mm Hg. Physical findings included facial swelling, abdominal bloating, easy bruising, and significant weight gain (30 lbs).\n\n- Initial Imaging: Head, neck, and chest imaging showed a 2.4-cm parotid mass and a 2.7-cm level IIB lymph node.- Fine-needle aspiration biopsy: Showed features of a salivary gland neoplasm with atypia.- Laboratory Tests (18 months after presentation):  * Serum cortisol: 1151 nmol/L (normal range: 133-537)  * ACTH level: 78.5 pmol/L (normal range: 1.6-13.9)  * Random blood glucose: 16 mmol/L (elevated)  * Potassium: 3.0 mmol/L (normal range: 3.5-5.0)  * Aspartate aminotransferase: 62 IU/L (normal: <33)  * Alanine aminotransferase: 51 IU/L (normal: <32)  * 24-hour urine cortisol: 1375 mmol/d (normal: <275) on day 1, 1228 mmol/d on day 2  * Midnight salivary cortisol: 68.6 and 59.7 nmol/L (normal: <11.3) on two consecutive nights- Histological Examination: Stage T2N2B, with metastases in 5 of 47 lymph nodes, lymphovascular and perineural invasion- Immunohistochemical staining: Positive for ACTH in 30-40% of invasive tumor cells; negative for synaptophysin and chromogranin",
    "frontier": [
